    <statement><p>Find the linear approximation to <m>f(x,y,z)=\frac{xy}{z}</m> at the point <m>(-2,-1,-1)</m>:</p>
<p><m>f(x,y,z)\approx</m><fillin characters="20" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>x+2y-2z</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><m>(-2,-1,-1)</m>:

<me>\left. \begin{array}{lcl}
f(x,y,z)=\frac{xy}{z} \amp \amp  f(-2,-1,-1)=-2 \\
f_x(x,y,z)=\frac{y}{z} \amp  \Rightarrow \amp  f_x(-2,-1,-1)=1 \\
f_y(x,y,z)=\frac{x}{z}  \amp \amp  f_y(-2,-1,-1)=2 \\
f_z(x,y,z)=-\frac{xy}{z^{2}} \amp \amp  f_z(-2,-1,-1)=-2
\end{array} \right.</me>

The linear approximation is therefore
<me>f(x,y,z)\approx f(-2,-1,-1)+f_x(-2,-1,-1)(x + 2)+</me><me>f_y(-2,-1,-1)(y + 1)+f_z(-2,-1,-1)(z + 1)</me>
<me>f(x,y,z)\approx x+2-2+2\mathopen{}\left(y+1\right)-2\mathopen{}\left(z+1\right)</me>
<me>f(x,y,z)\approx x+2y-2z</me>





</solution>
